WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
On the Home screen, click Open project… and choose the project of the tutorial Repair instead of cropping.

2
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.000
The project opens up to date: nothing is to be computed.

3
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:11.000
Click Post.

4
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Before any gesture: the Synthetic stars row has no mask, so the synthetic stars replace all the real ones, and a veil is left on the sky.

5
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Click the mask thumbnail of the Synthetic stars row: the mask is empty, the row shows everywhere.

6
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Click Add to mask: a mask is made of parts of five kinds.

7
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Choose Brush: Subtract and Hide are set, so painting hides the row.

8
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.000
Drag Size to the far right: as large as it goes, here 1,200 px.

9
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:42.000
Drag Hardness to 0: the edge of the stroke fades out.

10
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:48.000
Paint one stroke across the centre of the field: the real stars come back there.

11
00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:53.000
Press M to see the mask in grey: white shows the synthetic stars, black hides them.

12
00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:57.000
Whole mask, Feather: drag it right to soften the edge of the mask, to see.

13
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:01.000
Density: lower it and the hidden part lets the row through again, to see.

14
00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Tick Invert: the synthetic stars now show at the centre only, to see.

15
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Untick it to go back.

16
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Click Show, then drag across the sky: this stroke gives the synthetic stars back there.

17
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Press Ctrl+Z: the last stroke is undone.

18
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Click Clear all: every stroke goes, and the row shows everywhere again. Ctrl+Z takes it back.

19
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Press Ctrl+Z: the brush mask is back.

20
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:28.000
Click Starless image.

21
00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
Click Filter, open Contrast and choose Dehaze.

22
00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:37.000
Drag Amount to about 0.25: the veil of the sky goes.

23
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:41.000
Drag Scale to about 256 px.

24
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:45.000
With the filter selected, click the mask button under the list.

25
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.000
Click Add to mask and choose Luminance range.

26
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:52.000
Drag From to 0 %.

27
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:57.000
Drag To to 25 %: the part selects the sky and the faint tones, not the ring.

28
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Drag Feather to about 8 px.

29
00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Press M: white is where Dehaze acts, on the dark tones.

30
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:11.000
Press Ctrl+S: the masks are saved with the project.

31
00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Open File, then click Close project.

32
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Click the project to open it again.

33
00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:23.000
The masks are still there: a click on a thumbnail opens its parts, still editable.
